<h1><span lang="EN-GB">Undo</span></h1>
<hr align="left" width="98.974%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span lang="EN-GB">This command is accessible from</span> <i style="font-style:normal;"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Edit &gt; Undo</b></span></i> <span lang="EN-GB">or by pressing</span> <span class="Shortcut" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Ctrl + Z</b></span><span lang="EN-GB">.</span></p>
<p class="description"><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span> <span class="Command">Undo</span> <span lang="EN-GB">command restores the previous state of data in the scene. It works one step back only. The command is grayed when the Enable Undo command is not checked. You cannot undo File operations and operations in Inspection mode.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB">The</span> <span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Undo</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">command is enabled by default.</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>How the command works</b></span><span lang="EN-GB">: When the Undo command is enabled, the current state of the scene is saved with .ktf (KUBE Temp File) extension in the user's temporary folder ("System_drive\Documents and Settings\User\Local Settings\Temp"). The procedure is repeated before executing each operation. That leads to slowing down a little bit the data processing, especially when working with big files. If you need to undo multiple operations, then use the Hold and Fetch functions.</span></p>
<p class="Use"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Use</b></span>: This command is very useful when needed to reject the last data modification in a file.</p>
